  "narrative": "## Biological Interpretation\n\nThe metabolite list is overwhelmingly enriched for **tryptophan metabolism** (KEGG map00380; FDR ≈ 0, fold-enrichment ≈ 584), which is by far the dominant signal. Seven of the twelve differentially-abundant metabolites map directly onto this pathway: L-tryptophan, indoleacetaldehyde, tryptamine, serotonin, 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid, quinolinic acid, and melatonin. A second highly significant pathway, **biogenic amine synthesis** (Wikipathways WP550; FDR ≈ 0, fold-enrichment ≈ 1060), is also enriched and overlaps substantially with the tryptophan-derived neuroactive intermediates.\n\n### Key Driver Compounds\n\n**L-Tryptophan (C00078)** is the entry-point driver. KEGG reaction-graph traversal confirms it is the metabolic precursor of the serotonin–melatonin axis: L-tryptophan → serotonin → melatonin (4-hop forward path). This canonical route passes through tryptamine and indoleacetaldehyde as verified intermediates, linking all three neuroactive metabolites in a single directional module. **3-Hydroxyanthranilic acid (C00632)** anchors the kynurenine branch of tryptophan catabolism, confirmed downstream of L-tryptophan via a 4-hop path, and is annotated in NAD⁺ biosynthetic pathways (WP3645 / WP2485). **Quinolinic acid (C03722)** occupies the same kynurenine-to-NAD⁺ segment (KEGG: nicotinate and nicotinamide metabolism) but was not reachable from L-tryptophan within the 6-hop BFS budget, suggesting it may branch through an intermediate not captured in this differential list. **Acetylcholine (C01996)** contributes to the biogenic amine synthesis enrichment independently.\n\n### Biological Significance\n\nThe coordinated perturbation of the tryptophan–serotonin–melatonin axis suggests altered tryptophan catabolism toward either the neurotransmitter or the kynurenine–NAD⁺ branch. Elevated serotonin, melatonin, and their immediate precursors indicate enhanced indole/serotonin biosynthesis, which would impact mood regulation, circadian rhythm, and immune modulation. The simultaneous increase in 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid and quinolinic acid points to activation of the kynurenine pathway, a major route for NAD⁺ precursor generation with known pro-oxidant and neurotoxic properties when elevated. This dual activation is biologically significant because tryptophan is a limited substrate shared between two functionally opposing branches; a treatment-driven shift would alter serotonin:NAD⁺ flux ratios with downstream consequences for neuronal energy metabolism and oxidative stress. Notably, **2,3-epoxyaflatoxin B1** (C19586) — a mycotoxin reactive intermediate — was also detected, raising the possibility that xenobiotic exposure is the upstream trigger perturbing tryptophan catabolism.",
